      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $b_{21}=-5$ is in the same column as the leading entry $b_{11}=9$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $c_{43}=2$ is in the same column as the leading entry $c_{33}=-9$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & -8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is in row echelon form.",
